{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/*.md; do printf '\\\\n### %s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n \"Acceptance Criteria|Implementation Plan|Anti-Shim|Spec Deviations|Open Questions|Definition of Done|Status|Out of Scope|Scope|Requirements|AC-|TC-|done|Deviation|ruling\" \"$f\" | head -n 120; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/*.md; do printf '\\\\n### %s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n \"Acceptance Criteria|Implementation Plan|Anti-Shim|Spec Deviations|Open Questions|Definition of Done|Status|Out of Scope|Scope|Requirements|AC-|TC-|done|Deviation|ruling\" \"$f\" | head -n 120; done'","aggregated_output":"\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n23:**Scope Out:**\n31:### Acceptance Criteria\n32:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n101:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n118:#### Spec Deviations\n122:### Definition of Done\n123:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n24:**Scope Out:**\n32:### Acceptance Criteria\n33:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n35:**AC-1.1:** On `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag, the extension creates a new LHC thread (registering it in the catalog with its cwd), initializes one LHC instance in background scheduler mode against it, and does not drive the derivation queue itself.\n37:**TC-1.1** — Fresh `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag: one LHC instance initialized in background mode, one new registry thread created with cwd/title metadata, and no PI-session-to-thread mapping record created.\n39:**AC-1.2:** On `session_start` that resolves to an existing thread (via `--continue`, `--session`, or a picker selection), the extension initializes against that thread by resolving it through the registry. No second thread is created for an already-resolved thread.\n41:**TC-1.2** — `session_start` with launch input resolving an existing registry thread: initializes against that same thread and creates no second thread.\n43:**AC-1.3:** Across all hooks, the extension holds only plain data (thread reference, file path, told-the-user flags) between events. It retains no PI context or session-manager object across hook boundaries; each handler uses the fresh context PI provides. A session replacement (new/resume/fork) that invalidates prior context objects does not break capture.\n45:**TC-1.3** — A session replacement invalidates the prior context object; the extension, holding only plain data, continues capture using the fresh context.\n47:**AC-1.4:** On `session_shutdown`, the extension disposes the LHC instance with flush and cleanup. A subsequent run that resolves the same thread finds it complete through the last event recorded before shutdown — no trailing loss.\n49:**TC-1.4** — `session_shutdown` disposes with flush; reattach finds the thread complete through the last pre-shutdown event.\n51:**AC-1.5:** On reload (extension torn down and re-initialized while the same session continues), the extension reconstructs the thread reference from durable state (the resolved thread id), not from retained in-memory objects, and capture continues on the same thread.\n53:**TC-1.5** — Reload re-initializes the extension; thread reference reconstructed from the resolved thread id; capture continues on the same thread.\n55:**AC-1.6:** Launch resolves the thread by mode: no flag creates a new thread; `--session <id>` resolves a named thread by full or partial id; `--continue`/`-c` resolves the most recently created thread; an ambiguous or unresolvable id fails with an actionable message rather than silently creating a new thread.\n57:**TC-1.6** — Launch by mode resolves correctly: no flag creates a new thread; `--session` resolves by full and by partial id; `--continue` resolves the most recent; an ambiguous/unresolvable id fails with an actionable message and creates no thread.\n59:**AC-1.7:** `--resume`/`-r` lists threads scoped to the current working directory, each shown with its title and creation time, and resolves the operator's selection. With no threads for the cwd, the picker reports an empty list rather than failing.\n61:**TC-1.7** — `--resume` lists cwd-scoped threads with title and creation time and resolves a selection; an empty cwd lists nothing rather than failing.\n112:| TC-1.1 | `test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts` | Fresh launch creates one cwd/titled registry thread and one background LHC instance; connector does not drive the queue. |\n113:| TC-1.2 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Existing launch resolves through registry and creates no second thread. |\n114:| TC-1.3 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Fresh PI context objects across hooks do not break capture; retained holder contains no prior context object. |\n115:| TC-1.4 | `test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts` | Shutdown flushes; re-resolving the thread sees every pre-shutdown event. |\n116:| TC-1.5 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Reload discards in-memory holder, resolves the same thread id from registry, and continues on that thread. |\n117:| TC-1.6 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | No flag, full id, partial id, continue, and bad id resolve exactly as specified; bad id creates no thread. |\n118:| TC-1.7 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Resume picker lists only current-cwd threads with title and creation time; empty cwd returns an empty selection state. |\n132:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n149:#### Spec Deviations\n153:### Definition of Done\n154:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n157:- LHC registry supports the A-8 fields and operations needed by AC-1.6 and AC-1.7.\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n25:**Scope Out:**\n34:### Acceptance Criteria\n35:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n37:**AC-2.1:** Every PI message finalized through `message_end` — user, assistant, tool result — is mapped to LHC intake events and recorded in source order. An assistant message fans out to per-content-part events in thinking → text → tool-call order: `assistant_thinking`, `assistant_text`, and one `tool_call` per call.\n39:**TC-2.1** — A user/assistant/toolResult sequence records ordered events; an assistant `[thinking, text, toolCall]` fans out in that order.\n41:**AC-2.2:** LHC turn boundaries are derived from PI traffic, not from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`. One LHC turn spans a user prompt and all subsequent agent activity until the next user prompt or the end of the agent run. The converter emits exactly one LHC `turn_end` event per LHC turn, at the agent run's completion — never one per PI `turn_end`, and never keyed off PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex` as a session counter.\n43:**TC-2.2** — A one-prompt-two-tools-then-answer corpus (the worked example) yields exactly one LHC turn with one `turn_end` at agent-run completion; PI's per-step `turn_end`s produce none.\n45:**TC-2.3** — Session order derives from converter source-event order, not `turnIndex`; two agent runs each starting at `turnIndex 0` order correctly.\n47:**AC-2.3:** Parallel tool calls are captured with correct correlation: when one assistant message carries multiple `tool_call` parts and their results complete out of arrival order, each `tool_result` event is matched to its call by `toolCallId`, not by arrival order.\n49:**TC-2.4** — Parallel tool calls with out-of-order completion correlate each result to its call by `toolCallId`.\n51:**AC-2.4:** A tool result carrying an error is captured as a `tool_result` event with its error content and an error flag set. No event is dropped because a tool failed.\n53:**TC-2.5** — A tool result with `isError` is captured as a `tool_result` with the error flag and content; nothing dropped.\n55:**AC-2.5:** A graceful interrupt — a complete turn PI marks aborted, with partial assistant content preserved — is captured whole: the partial content is recorded and the aborted disposition is carried through. The interrupted content is not discarded.\n57:**TC-2.6** — A graceful-interrupt corpus records the partial assistant content with the aborted disposition; the turn closes complete-but-aborted.\n59:**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\n61:**TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\n63:**AC-2.7:** A capture failure does not break the PI session, and does not vanish silently. When the thread is writable, a malformed or unmappable event records a durable, queryable gap that surfaces in thread health. When the thread store itself is unavailable, the failure surfaces as an extension diagnostic / health signal rather than a durable gap (the record cannot be written), and no exception propagates into the PI hook. Either way the session continues, and capture adds no perceptible latency to interactive use.\n65:**TC-2.8** — A malformed/unmappable event on a writable thread records a durable, queryable gap surfaced in health; an unavailable thread store surfaces an extension diagnostic / health signal with no durable gap. Both continue the session with no exception reaching the hook.\n67:**AC-2.8:** Runtime changes that PI fires only in-stream — model selection (`model_select`) and thinking-level selection (`thinking_level_select`) — are captured in order as `runtime_note` events carrying the change (the new model or level, and the previous one). These are recorded at the moment they fire because no durable record holds them otherwise; nothing in this epic consumes them, and their presence in the thread is what lets later epics attribute a turn to the model that produced it.\n69:**TC-2.9** — A `model_select` and a `thinking_level_select` each record an ordered `runtime_note` event carrying the new and previous values; ordering relative to surrounding messages is preserved.\n120:| TC-2.1 | `test/capture/converter.test.ts` | User, assistant, and tool-result sequence records in source order; assistant fan-out is thinking, text, then tool calls. |\n121:| TC-2.2 | `test/capture/turn-derivation.test.ts` | Worked example produces exactly one LHC turn and one `turn_end` at `agent_end`; PI per-step `turn_end`s emit none. |\n122:| TC-2.3 | `test/capture/turn-derivation.test.ts` | Two agent runs with repeated `turnIndex` values order by converter source-event order without collision. |\n123:| TC-2.4 | `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts` | Out-of-order parallel tool results correlate to calls by `toolCallId`. |\n124:| TC-2.5 | `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts` | Error tool result records error flag and content; no failed tool output is dropped. |\n125:| TC-2.6 | `test/capture/abort.test.ts` | Graceful interrupt records partial assistant content, carries aborted disposition, and closes complete-but-aborted. |\n126:| TC-2.7 | `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts` | Re-delivered event is skipped by idempotency key; normal resume does not replay historical messages. |\n127:| TC-2.8 | `test/capture/converter.test.ts` | Writable malformed event records durable gap; unavailable store records state health signal with no hook exception. |\n128:| TC-2.9 | `test/capture/converter.test.ts` | Model and thinking-level changes become ordered `runtime_note` events with new and previous values. |\n144:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n162:#### Spec Deviations\n166:### Definition of Done\n167:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n22:**Scope Out:**\n30:### Acceptance Criteria\n31:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n33:**AC-6.1:** Replaying a recorded PI corpus through the converter produces a thread whose read-back — events, messages, and turns — matches the fixture's expectation, including the chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, and aborted-turn corpora.\n35:**TC-6.1** — Each corpus (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn) replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture.\n37:**AC-6.2:** Replay is deterministic: the same corpus replayed twice produces identical thread read-back. The deterministic-ID property holds through the converter, so a re-replay does not perturb IDs or ordering.\n39:**TC-6.2** — The same corpus replayed twice yields identical read-back (IDs and order stable).\n41:**AC-6.3:** inspect surfaces (overview, health) reflect the captured session: event and message counts, the last recorded event position, and any capture gaps or failed derivations are visible rather than hidden.\n43:**TC-6.3** — inspect overview/health report event/message counts, last event position, and any gaps or failed derivations.\n91:| TC-6.1 | `test/verify/replay.test.ts` | Each available recorded corpus replays through the converter to a thread whose events, messages, and turns match fixture expectation. |\n92:| TC-6.2 | `test/verify/replay.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice yields identical read-back, including deterministic IDs and order. |\n93:| TC-6.3 | `test/verify/replay.test.ts` | Inspect overview/health report event/message counts, last recorded position, gaps, and failed derivations. |\n107:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n124:#### Spec Deviations\n128:### Definition of Done\n129:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/04-fork-as-new-thread.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n23:**Scope Out:**\n31:### Acceptance Criteria\n32:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n34:**AC-3.1:** Forking a PI session creates a new LHC thread. The source thread receives no writes — it is unchanged by the fork.\n36:**TC-3.1** — Fork creates a new thread; the source thread receives no writes — its logical read-back (events, messages, turns) is unchanged. Detection resolves the fork point from the `session_before_fork` hook, with PI's session tree as available fallback evidence in Epic 1, not from a fork reason code.\n38:**AC-3.2:** The new thread is seeded by replaying the source thread's recorded events up to the fork point. The seeded thread's read-back (events, messages, turns) matches the source's read-back through that point.\n40:**TC-3.2** — Fork replay seeds the new thread; read-back matches the source through the fork point.\n42:**AC-3.3:** Derived forms may be reused from the source thread when provenance identity proves the reuse safe; when safety cannot be proven, the affected derivations requeue on the new thread. The forked thread's read-back is correct under either path.\n44:**TC-3.3** — Fork with safe provenance reuses derived forms; fork with unprovable provenance requeues them; both yield a correct thread.\n91:| TC-3.1 | `test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts` | Fork creates a new thread, resolves the fork point from hook data with Epic 1 fallback evidence, and leaves source logical read-back unchanged. |\n92:| TC-3.2 | `test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts` | Fork target is seeded by replay and read-back matches the source through the fork point. |\n93:| TC-3.3 | `test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts` | V1 requeues forms on the fork target and does not copy source derived forms; the target read-back remains correct. |\n107:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n125:#### Spec Deviations\n129:### Definition of Done\n130:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/05-inference-host-routing.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n23:**Scope Out:**\n29:**Dependencies:** Story 1 for initialized LHC instance. Story 2 for captured content used by AC-4.5.\n31:### Acceptance Criteria\n32:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n34:**AC-4.1:** The extension supplies one model-call function to the LHC instance at initialization. Given a (provider, model) pair and a system/user message list, the function resolves the pair through PI's model registry and auth and returns either the completion text or a classified failure.\n36:**TC-4.1** — The model-call function resolves a (provider, model) pair through PI's registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list.\n38:**AC-4.2:** Different derivation kinds may route to different (provider, model) pairs within the same session. The function routes each call by its provided keys; LHC does not interpret the keys.\n40:**TC-4.2** — Two derivation kinds assigned to different (provider, model) pairs both route correctly in one session.\n42:**AC-4.3:** Provider errors map to LHC's failure classification: auth and invalid-request are terminal; rate-limit, timeout, and network are retryable. A thrown exception classifies as the generic `other` kind.\n44:**TC-4.3** — Auth and invalid-request map to terminal; rate-limit, timeout, network map to retryable; a thrown exception maps to `other`.\n46:**AC-4.4:** A call that resolves but produces no output is classified as `empty_output` by the LHC adapter. The host's function never returns `empty_output` itself — it returns text or a transport/auth failure.\n48:**TC-4.4** — A resolved call with no output is classified `empty_output` by the adapter; the host function returns text-or-transport-failure only.\n50:**AC-4.5:** A captured thread's background derivation work invokes the injected model-call function and records a result against the thread: a queued derivation runs through LHC's scheduler, calls the function, and persists either a ready derived form or a classified failure, queryable through inspect/health. This closes the loop end to end — capture to derivation to recorded outcome — not just the function in isolation.\n52:**TC-4.5** — A captured thread with queued derivation work, drained through LHC's scheduler with the injected function wired, persists at least one ready derived form (function returns text) and one classified failure (function returns a failure), both queryable through inspect/health — proving the capture→derivation→recorded-outcome loop, not just the function in isolation.\n100:| TC-4.1 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Function resolves provider/model via PI registry and returns completion text for a single-turn message list. |\n101:| TC-4.2 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Two derivation kinds route to different provider/model pairs in one run and both succeed. |\n102:| TC-4.3 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Auth and invalid-request classify terminal; rate-limit, timeout, and network classify retryable; thrown exception maps to `other`. |\n103:| TC-4.4 | `test/inference/model-call.test.ts` | Resolved no-output completion becomes `empty_output` at the LHC adapter; host function returns text or transport/auth failure only. |\n104:| TC-4.5 | `test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts` | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes injected function and persists one ready form plus one classified failure queryable through inspect/health. |\n119:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n137:#### Spec Deviations\n141:### Definition of Done\n142:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md\n15:**Scope In:**\n24:**Scope Out:**\n27:- Status-bar/footer UX beyond actionable startup-validation reporting.\n32:### Acceptance Criteria\n33:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n35:**AC-5.1:** At session start the extension validates all seven model assignments against PI's registry before first derivation use.\n37:**TC-5.1** — All seven assignments validate against the registry at session start before first use.\n39:**AC-5.2:** An unreachable lane — not logged in, unknown model, unknown provider — is reported before first use, naming the derivation kind, the (provider, model), and the corrective action. The report reaches the user in interactive and headless modes alike (guarded on UI availability, never assuming a TUI).\n41:**TC-5.2** — An unreachable lane reports kind + (provider, model) + fix; the report appears in a headless mode, not only the TUI.\n43:**AC-5.3:** A validation failure leaves capture running. Derivations on the affected lanes fail, classified and queryable through health; the session is not broken.\n45:**TC-5.3** — A validation failure leaves capture running; the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable.\n47:**AC-5.4:** Model assignments load from the extension's config. Each of the seven derivation kinds resolves to a (provider, model, prompt), where the prompt names a registered prompt. The epic ships with default assignments so derivations function; assignment quality is a dial-in concern, not a build gate.\n49:**TC-5.4** — Each of the seven kinds loads a (provider, model, prompt) from config with shipped defaults; the prompt resolves to a registered prompt.\n51:**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\n53:**TC-5.5** — A user override of a kind's provider/model/prompt takes effect on the next session start with no code change.\n55:**TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\n102:| TC-5.1 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | All seven assignments validate at start before first derivation use. |\n103:| TC-5.2 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | Unreachable lane report names derivation kind, provider/model, reason, and fix; report appears in headless mode and state health. |\n104:| TC-5.3 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | Validation failure leaves capture running; affected derivations fail classified and queryable. |\n105:| TC-5.4 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | Shipped defaults load provider/model/prompt for all seven kinds and prompts resolve. |\n106:| TC-5.5 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | User override of provider/model/prompt takes effect on next start. |\n107:| TC-5.6 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | Missing kind, unknown prompt, incomplete assignment, or placeholder fails loudly at initialization. |\n119:#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n137:#### Spec Deviations\n141:### Definition of Done\n142:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n\n### docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md\n13:### Acceptance Criteria\n14:<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n20:| AC-1.1 | Story 1 | TC-1.1 | Covered |\n21:| AC-1.2 | Story 1 | TC-1.2 | Covered |\n22:| AC-1.3 | Story 1 | TC-1.3 | Covered |\n23:| AC-1.4 | Story 1 | TC-1.4 | Covered |\n24:| AC-1.5 | Story 1 | TC-1.5 | Covered |\n25:| AC-1.6 | Story 1 | TC-1.6 | Covered |\n26:| AC-1.7 | Story 1 | TC-1.7 | Covered |\n27:| AC-2.1 | Story 2 | TC-2.1 | Covered |\n28:| AC-2.2 | Story 2 | TC-2.2, TC-2.3 | Covered |\n29:| AC-2.3 | Story 2 | TC-2.4 | Covered |\n30:| AC-2.4 | Story 2 | TC-2.5 | Covered |\n31:| AC-2.5 | Story 2 | TC-2.6 | Covered |\n32:| AC-2.6 | Story 2 | TC-2.7 | Covered |\n33:| AC-2.7 | Story 2 | TC-2.8 | Covered |\n34:| AC-2.8 | Story 2 | TC-2.9 | Covered |\n35:| AC-3.1 | Story 4 | TC-3.1 | Covered |\n36:| AC-3.2 | Story 4 | TC-3.2 | Covered |\n37:| AC-3.3 | Story 4 | TC-3.3 | Covered |\n38:| AC-4.1 | Story 5 | TC-4.1 | Covered |\n39:| AC-4.2 | Story 5 | TC-4.2 | Covered |\n40:| AC-4.3 | Story 5 | TC-4.3 | Covered |\n41:| AC-4.4 | Story 5 | TC-4.4 | Covered |\n42:| AC-4.5 | Story 5 | TC-4.5 | Covered |\n43:| AC-5.1 | Story 6 | TC-5.1 | Covered |\n44:| AC-5.2 | Story 6 | TC-5.2 | Covered |\n45:| AC-5.3 | Story 6 | TC-5.3 | Covered |\n46:| AC-5.4 | Story 6 | TC-5.4 | Covered |\n47:| AC-5.5 | Story 6 | TC-5.5, TC-5.6 | Covered |\n48:| AC-6.1 | Story 3 | TC-6.1 | Covered |\n49:| AC-6.2 | Story 3 | TC-6.2 | Covered |\n50:| AC-6.3 | Story 3 | TC-6.3 | Covered |\n59:| Fresh launch with no thread flag creates a registry thread and initializes LHC in background mode | Story 1 | TC-1.1 | Covered |\n60:| Existing launch modes resolve through registry without creating a second thread | Story 1 | TC-1.2, TC-1.6, TC-1.7 | Covered |\n61:| Extension survives fresh PI context objects and reload reconstruction | Story 1 | TC-1.3, TC-1.5 | Covered |\n62:| Shutdown flushes and disposes so next run sees complete thread content | Story 1 | TC-1.4 | Covered |\n63:| PI messages become ordered intake events with assistant content fan-out | Story 2 | TC-2.1 | Covered |\n64:| PI per-step turns collapse into one LHC turn per exchange | Story 2 | TC-2.2, TC-2.3 | Covered |\n65:| Parallel tools, tool errors, and graceful interrupts are captured | Story 2 | TC-2.4, TC-2.5, TC-2.6 | Covered |\n66:| Reload/replay dedup and capture failure isolation preserve session continuity | Story 2 | TC-2.7, TC-2.8 | Covered |\n67:| Runtime model and thinking-level changes are recorded in order | Story 2 | TC-2.9 | Covered |\n68:| Corpus replay proves capture read-back and deterministic IDs/order | Story 3 | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 | Covered |\n69:| Inspect overview/health exposes counts, position, gaps, and failed derivations | Story 3 | TC-6.3 | Covered |\n70:| Fork creates a new thread, seeds by replay, and never writes the source | Story 4 | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 | Covered |\n71:| Forked derivations reuse or requeue by provenance while preserving correctness | Story 4 | TC-3.3 | Covered |\n72:| LHC calls route through PI registry/auth with provider/model as host-interpreted keys | Story 5 | TC-4.1, TC-4.2 | Covered |\n73:| Provider failures and empty output classify under LHC contracts | Story 5 | TC-4.3, TC-4.4 | Covered |\n74:| Captured thread queued derivations persist ready forms and classified failures | Story 5 | TC-4.5 | Covered |\n75:| Startup validates all seven assignments before first derivation use | Story 6 | TC-5.1 | Covered |\n76:| Unreachable lanes report actionable fixes in interactive and headless modes | Story 6 | TC-5.2 | Covered |\n77:| Validation failures leave capture running and failures queryable | Story 6 | TC-5.3 | Covered |\n78:| Defaults and user overrides load for all seven derivation kinds | Story 6 | TC-5.4, TC-5.5, TC-5.6 | Covered |\n87:| Story 1: Session Lifecycle and Thread Resolution | foundation / invariant + adapter / mapping | The right thread is resolved from launch input and found again across reload and restart through the registry. | Registry additions plus lifecycle behavior | A-8 registry work is prerequisite scope for AC-1.6/1.7; avoid PI-session-to-thread mapping | Keep together because lifecycle init/dispose and thread resolution share the same thread identity invariant |\n91:| Story 5: Inference Host Routing | adapter / mapping + orchestration / convergence | LHC derivations reach PI logins through one function and persist queryable outcomes against the thread. | Function routing plus closed-loop proof | AC-4.5 depends on captured content from Story 2 | Keep together because the story proves both the host seam and the recorded derivation outcome |\n109:### Definition of Done\n110:<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
