{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/003-epic-review.json'","aggregated_output":"{\"command\":\"epic-review\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"blocked\",\"outcome\":\"block\",\"result\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"canonicalReview\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"reviewerLabels\":[\"epic-reviewer-2\"],\"reconciliationSummary\":\"Canonical review uses the single reviewer result from epic-reviewer-2.\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"Stories 2, 4, 5, and 6 are mostly coherent: capture, turn derivation, fork replay, inference routing, and startup validation all run through connector entry points and real LHC surfaces.\",\"Story 1 thread identity drift remains material: the current code both adds a PI custom-entry thread pointer and still keeps a cwd-most-recent fallback, while the epic and tech design describe launch/registry resolution with no PI-session-to-thread mapping.\",\"Story 3 does not meet the epic-level verification contract because the corpus loaders are synthetic stand-ins rather than recorded PI corpora.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Epic 1 correctly remains observe-only: index.ts registers no context hook and capture does not alter what PI serves to the model.\",\"The converter architecture matches the tech design: map-message is pure, turn-accumulator owns the LHC turn boundary, and converter writes through intakeStream.messageEvents.\",\"The current reload implementation conflicts with the tech design resolution of I-1/I-7: tech-design.md says there is no stored PI-session-to-thread pointer, but index.ts writes and reads a pi-lhc-thread-state custom entry via appendEntry/sessionManager.\",\"The production --resume default does not resolve an operator selection. It notifies the candidate list, auto-resolves only one candidate, and returns null for multiple candidates.\",\"Fork seeding uses the real intake path and source read-back is not written, but the fork path uses a filePath ThreadRef for the target, which is another sign that lifecycle thread identity is not consistently registry-id based.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"Flow 1 is incomplete against the written architecture and AC-1.7: launch resolution exists, but reload identity and resume selection still require human ruling or design update.\",\"Flow 2 is covered by source and tests: ordered message mapping, turn derivation, runtime notes, idempotency, gaps, parallel tools, errors, and aborts are implemented.\",\"Flow 3 is implemented with replay seeding and derived-form requeue; source immutability is covered.\",\"Flow 4 is implemented through createModelCall over PI modelRegistry plus pi-ai dynamic import, with classified failures and closed-loop persisted outcomes covered.\",\"Flow 5 is implemented: assignments load defaults/overrides, startup validation reports unreachable lanes, and capture continues.\",\"Flow 6 is not fully covered: replay is deterministic and compares real read-back, but it consumes synthetic fixtures instead of recorded PI corpora required by AC-6.1.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapter was found in src capture/fork/inspect paths: capture uses real LHC intakeStream, fork replay uses intakeStream.messageEvents, and inspect reads real LHC surfaces.\",\"Test-only model fakes are confined to test/fixtures and are not production code.\",\"createModelCall does not fabricate output when pi-ai is missing; it returns a classified failure.\",\"Material non-real verification path: packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts explicitly says loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers recordings, so Flow 6 is not proving recorded PI corpus replay.\",\"Material architecture drift: production index.ts persists threadRef in PI custom entries, despite tech-design.md stating the mapping persistence problem is dissolved and no session-file pointer is written.\",\"Material resume limitation: production defaultSelectThread has no interactive operator selection path for multiple cwd-scoped threads.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"EV-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Capture verification uses synthetic stand-ins instead of recorded PI corpora\",\"evidence\":\"epic.md:69 and epic.md:229-231 require recorded corpora replay through the converter. packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:1-5 states the loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers real recordings; loadAllCorpora at lines 137-149 returns only those synthetic loaders.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/verify/replay.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-6.1\",\"TC-6.1\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"EV-002\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Production --resume does not resolve an operator selection\",\"evidence\":\"AC-1.7 in epic.md:141 requires --resume to resolve the operator's selection. index.ts:210-226 only notifies the candidate list and returns the single candidate or null for multiple candidates; thread-resolution.test.ts:255-292 asserts fail-closed behavior for multiple candidates rather than a selected thread.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/picker.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.7\",\"TC-1.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"EV-003\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Reload identity reintroduced a PI custom-entry mapping contrary to the tech design\",\"evidence\":\"tech-design.md:22 says there is no PI-session-to-thread mapping to persist, and tech-design.md:26 says appendEntry is moot for mapping because no session-file pointer is written. index.ts:134-158 defines pi-lhc-thread-state, index.ts:304-306 reads it from ctx.sessionManager.getEntries(), index.ts:468-469 appends t