ectly conflicts with Reviewer 2's assessment that these files lack PI hook metadata. I need to examine the actual corpus files to determine which reviewer's assessment is accurate. The discrepancy centers on whether the test data genuinely represents real PI hook/lifecycle events or is merely simplified message data.\n\nFor EV-002, both reviewers identify the same reload behavior—using cwd-most-recent instead of exact-thread resolution. Reviewer 1 treats this as a documented design constraint with NF-002 as an observation, while Reviewer 2 elevates it to a blocking issue, asserting it violates AC-1.5. The core tension is whether this represents an accepted architectural limitation or a requirement breach.\n\nThe critical question is whether the design explicitly permits cwd-most-recent reattachment as a fallback when exact-thread resolution isn't possible, or if AC-1.5 strictly demands exact-thread reconstruction regardless of feasibility.\n\nFor Story 3, Reviewer 1 characterizes the verification as \"deterministic with read-back compare,\" but Reviewer 2 argues this fails to satisfy the epic-level verification contract since the fixtures lack real PI lifecycle corpora. Even if replay mechanics work correctly, the underlying test data appears insufficient to validate actual behavior.\n\nThe fundamental dispute centers on scope: Reviewer 1 is checking whether the implementation matches its own spec, while Reviewer 2 is checking whether that spec adequately captures the epic's requirements.\n\nI need to examine the actual evidence to determine which finding holds. My reconciliation approach starts with verification of the blocking claims themselves—if Reviewer 2's blockers are substantiated by the codebase, they stand; otherwise, Reviewer 1's assessment prevails.\n\nReviewing the pattern from prior fix batches shows that downgrading blockers requires explicit disproof from both code and epic artifacts, not just differing perspectives between reviewers. The conservative principle favors maintaining the block unless I can definitively refute the blocking finding. Since I'm not independently auditing the entire codebase but rather validating the claimed issues, I need to determine whether these blockers are genuine defects or merely differences in interpretation. The question is whether these represent actual specification violations or just alternative valid implementations. I need to finish the partial thought and then complete the thought.\n\nLooking at the next thinking, I can see it continues the reconciliation summary, so I'll complete this thought by copying over the next thinking up to the newline:\n\nNF-002 as an observation about reload re-resolving from cwd-most-recent in registry due to design constraints. The discrepancy is whether this constitutes an accepted constraint or a blocking violation.\n\nThe key question is whether cwd-most-recent re-resolution is documented as a permitted design constraint. I need to check the tech design and epic artifacts for explicit text acknowledging this limitation. The registry-based approach appears intentional rather than accidental, suggesting it may be a documented architectural tradeoff rather than a defect.\n\nBoth EV-001 and EV-002 appear to be valid blockers since Reviewer 2 identifies substantive gaps: Story 3 fails to satisfy the epic-level verification contract because it uses simplified AgentMessage JSONL rows instead of recorded PI hook/lifecycle corpora, and the reload implementation conflicts with AC-1.5's requirement to reconstruct from the resolved threadId.\n\nThe output should include only the canonical findings once confirmed, merging any duplicate items from both reviewers while ensuring the JSON format contains exactly one object as specified in the output contract.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"757bb664-2d09-45c0-80b1-2c0b9